<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Skilurus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Skilurus was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in the 2nd century BCE who ruled from Scythian Neapolis near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simferopol">Simferopol</a>. He extended his power over the Greek city of Olbia, where coins were struck in his name, and pressed Chersonesus. A stone mausoleum at Neapolis holding many burials and rich grave goods is often attributed to him, and his sons, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palakus">Palakus</a>, continued the war against Chersonesus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/skilurus.jpg" alt="Skilurus" width="204" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Janmad · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skiluros.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
